Petri Net Alignment:
submitted by Massimiliano de Leoni (Eindhoven University of Technology) and
Andrea Marrella (Sapienza University of Rome)

Conformance checking compares the observed behavior of a process, recorded as
traces of events, with a process model given as a Petri net. An alignment
relates each event of a trace to a transition of the net. It consists of
synchronous moves (the trace and the net agree), moves in the log (an event
without a matching transition) and moves in the model (a transition without a
matching event). Moves in the log and moves in visible transitions cost 1,
synchronous moves and invisible transitions cost 0. The task is to find an
alignment with minimal cost.

References:

M. de Leoni and A. Marrella. Aligning Real Process Executions and Prescriptive
Process Models through Automated Planning. Expert Systems With Applications 82,
pages 162-183, 2017.

M. de Leoni and A. Marrella. How Planning Techniques Can Help Process Mining:
The Conformance-Checking Case. SEBD 2017.


Generator:

The original generator is a Java GUI ("Planning-based Imperative Conformance
Checker", https://bitbucket.org/ipc2018-classical/generate-petri-net-alignment)
that encodes the traces of an event log in XES format and a Petri net in PNML
format. For IPC 2018, the event log consisted of one trace of a synthetic log
for the net d64 with 0, 10, ..., 190 activities from other traces inserted at
random positions. In addition, each pair of adjacent events was swapped with
probability 30/101.

generate.py is a Python reimplementation of this pipeline. Instead of using a
given event log, it generates traces by firing random enabled transitions of
the net until no transition is enabled. The initial marking consists of the
places without incoming arcs and the final marking of the places without
outgoing arcs. For the traces of the IPC 2018 tasks, the encoding is identical
to the IPC 2018 tasks.

Usage:

    ./generate.py nets/d64.pnml --seed 0 --noise 50 --domain domain.pddl --problem problem.pddl

The directory nets contains the synthetic Petri nets that come with the
original generator. Since the domain depends on the trace, each task has its
own domain file. generate-ipc-instances.sh generates 20 tasks like the IPC 2018
tasks.
